1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,080
In 2024, engineers at the National Renewable Energy Laboratory

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,060
in Colorado reported on a test at megawatt scale.

3
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:11,167
They took electricity, turned it into hydrogen, stored

4
00:00:11,167 --> 00:00:14,324
it, and then turned it back into electricity.

5
00:00:14,826 --> 00:00:19,648
At full power, about 28 percent of the electricity came back.

6
00:00:19,598 --> 00:00:22,596
At low power, about 35.

7
00:00:23,096 --> 00:00:25,801
Now compare that with a battery.

8
00:00:25,751 --> 00:00:29,724
In 2019, the EIA found that America's grid batteries gave

9
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:33,698
back about 82 percent of the electricity put into them.

10
00:00:33,698 --> 00:00:37,014
That number is called round-trip efficiency.

11
00:00:37,534 --> 00:00:39,901
So why did the federal government

12
00:00:39,901 --> 00:00:42,267
commit billions of dollars to hydrogen?

13
00:00:42,217 --> 00:00:44,615
And why might the hydrogen that matters

14
00:00:44,615 --> 00:00:47,013
most already be on your dinner table?

15
00:00:47,532 --> 00:00:50,990
Both answers come down to one idea: hydrogen isn't

16
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:54,064
really a fuel problem, it's a where-do-you-use-it problem.

17
00:00:54,014 --> 00:00:56,911
So let's follow the electron, and see where turning

18
00:00:56,911 --> 00:00:59,486
it into a molecule is worth the trip.

19
00:01:00,452 --> 00:01:03,693
First, what hydrogen actually is.

20
00:01:03,643 --> 00:01:06,874
It's the most abundant element in the universe, but on

21
00:01:06,874 --> 00:01:10,105
Earth it comes combined with other elements: with oxygen in

22
00:01:10,105 --> 00:01:13,337
water, or with carbon in natural gas, coal and oil.

23
00:01:13,858 --> 00:01:16,328
So before we can use it, we have

24
00:01:16,328 --> 00:01:18,489
to pull it apart from those compounds.

25
00:01:18,439 --> 00:01:22,304
The EIA calls hydrogen an energy carrier, something that moves

26
00:01:22,304 --> 00:01:26,169
energy from one place to another, a bit like electricity.

27
00:01:26,688 --> 00:01:29,087
And carrying has a price.

28
00:01:29,037 --> 00:01:32,825
In the EIA's words, producing hydrogen requires more energy

29
00:01:32,825 --> 00:01:36,614
than hydrogen provides when it's converted to useful energy.

30
00:01:36,614 --> 00:01:39,947
So every use of hydrogen pays that toll somewhere, and it's

31
00:01:39,947 --> 00:01:42,978
worth keeping in mind for the rest of this video.

32
00:01:43,952 --> 00:01:48,616
America already makes plenty of it: about 10 million metric tons

33
00:01:48,616 --> 00:01:52,856
a year, according to the Energy Department's 2023 hydrogen strategy.

34
00:01:52,806 --> 00:01:56,806
Worldwide, the International Energy Agency counted almost 100

35
00:01:56,806 --> 00:02:00,306
million metric tons of demand in 2024.

36
00:02:00,814 --> 00:02:04,107
The Energy Department says about 95 percent of U.S.

37
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:07,399
hydrogen is made from natural gas, in large plants

38
00:02:07,399 --> 00:02:09,960
that use a process called steam-methane reforming.

39
00:02:10,428 --> 00:02:16,041
Methane and steam meet at 1,300 to 1,800 degrees Fahrenheit.

40
00:02:15,991 --> 00:02:19,939
Out come hydrogen and carbon monoxide, and a second step

41
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:23,887
turns that carbon monoxide into carbon dioxide and more hydrogen.

42
00:02:24,386 --> 00:02:27,168
That carbon dioxide adds up.

43
00:02:27,118 --> 00:02:31,881
The Energy Department's 2023 hydrogen strategy put U.S. hydrogen production at

44
00:02:31,881 --> 00:02:36,212
about 100 million metric tons of greenhouse gases a year.

45
00:02:36,712 --> 00:02:39,774
Capture that carbon and store it underground, and

46
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:42,453
people often call the result blue hydrogen.

47
00:02:42,403 --> 00:02:45,745
Let it go into the air, and it's called grey.

48
00:02:46,254 --> 00:02:49,947
The other main route is electrolysis: running an electric current

49
00:02:49,947 --> 00:02:53,270
through water to split it into hydrogen and oxygen.

50
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:57,583
With wind or solar power, that's usually called green hydrogen.

51
00:02:57,583 --> 00:03:01,639
The Energy Department itself doesn't use the color code.

52
00:03:02,156 --> 00:03:05,747
Either way, the IEA says low-emission hydrogen was still

53
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:09,338
less than 1 percent of global production in 2024.

54
00:03:10,252 --> 00:03:13,021
Here's where the electron comes in.

55
00:03:12,971 --> 00:03:18,124
The Energy Department's 2022 status figure for a typical electrolyzer system

56
00:03:18,124 --> 00:03:23,277
was about 55 kilowatt-hours of electricity for every kilogram of hydrogen.

57
00:03:23,778 --> 00:03:26,617
Only about 61 percent of that electricity's

58
00:03:26,617 --> 00:03:29,455
energy ends up stored in the hydrogen.

59
00:03:29,405 --> 00:03:33,563
The department's long-term targets are around 70 percent.

60
00:03:33,563 --> 00:03:36,178
Better, but still a loss.

61
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:39,564
Then the hydrogen has to be stored.

62
00:03:39,514 --> 00:03:42,208
It holds more energy per pound than any

63
00:03:42,208 --> 00:03:44,565
other fuel, but very little per gallon.

64
00:03:44,565 --> 00:03:48,700
So it gets squeezed into tanks at up to 10,000 psi,

65
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:52,460
or chilled into a liquid at minus 423 degrees Fahrenheit.

66
00:03:53,416 --> 00:03:55,764
Now stack the losses.

67
00:03:55,714 --> 00:03:58,864
That's the chain from the start of this

68
00:03:58,864 --> 00:04:02,015
video: electricity to hydrogen, then back to electricity.

69
00:04:02,015 --> 00:04:05,112
In that Colorado test, the fuel cell generator

70
00:04:05,112 --> 00:04:08,208
alone turned only about 48 to 52 percent

71
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:10,918
of the hydrogen's energy back into electricity.

72
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:14,441
A hydrogen car runs the same chain.

73
00:04:14,391 --> 00:04:16,917
Its fuel cell turns hydrogen back

74
00:04:16,917 --> 00:04:19,442
into electricity for an electric motor.

75
00:04:19,442 --> 00:04:24,174
A battery car skips the detour and stores the electricity directly.

76
00:04:24,692 --> 00:04:27,244
Now look at the fueling map.

77
00:04:27,194 --> 00:04:30,646
As of November 2026, the Energy Department's station

78
00:04:30,646 --> 00:04:34,097
locator lists 44 hydrogen fueling stations in the

79
00:04:34,097 --> 00:04:37,118
whole country, 42 of them in California.

80
00:04:37,118 --> 00:04:41,901
It lists nearly 84,000 charging locations for electric cars.

81
00:04:42,418 --> 00:04:46,118
Home heating has the same problem, only sharper.

82
00:04:46,068 --> 00:04:49,142
The Energy Department says a heat pump can deliver up

83
00:04:49,142 --> 00:04:52,216
to three times more heat than the energy it uses.

84
00:04:52,728 --> 00:04:56,185
Turn that same electricity into hydrogen, and nearly 40 percent

85
00:04:56,185 --> 00:04:59,642
of the energy is gone before you light a burner.

86
00:04:59,592 --> 00:05:02,862
Hydrogen also packs only about a third of natural gas's

87
00:05:02,862 --> 00:05:06,131
energy by volume, and the EIA says more research is

88
00:05:06,131 --> 00:05:09,401
needed to know whether home heating appliances can handle it.

89
00:05:09,902 --> 00:05:13,021
So for cars and homes, where electricity can do

90
00:05:13,021 --> 00:05:16,140
the job directly, hydrogen is the long way around.

91
00:05:17,062 --> 00:05:19,180
But flip the question.

92
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:23,518
What if you don't need energy, but the hydrogen atoms themselves?

93
00:05:23,518 --> 00:05:25,901
That's where it makes sense, not as

94
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:27,944
a fuel, but as an ingredient.

95
00:05:28,452 --> 00:05:30,864
Oil refineries are one.

96
00:05:30,814 --> 00:05:34,423
They use hydrogen to remove sulfur from fuels.

97
00:05:34,423 --> 00:05:37,857
Worldwide, refining used more than 43 million

98
00:05:37,857 --> 00:05:40,801
metric tons of hydrogen in 2024.

99
00:05:41,306 --> 00:05:43,960
Fertilizer is the big one.

100
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:47,676
Hydrogen plus nitrogen from the air makes ammonia, and in

101
00:05:47,676 --> 00:05:51,066
the U.S., 88 percent of ammonia goes into fertilizer.

102
00:05:51,066 --> 00:05:55,109
So in a sense, that's the hydrogen on your dinner table.

103
00:05:55,624 --> 00:05:58,373
Worldwide, ammonia takes about 60 percent

104
00:05:58,373 --> 00:06:01,122
of the hydrogen used in industry.

105
00:06:01,072 --> 00:06:04,682
Switch to clean hydrogen, and the Energy Department says ammonia

106
00:06:04,682 --> 00:06:08,292
plants could cut their emissions by more than 90 percent.

107
00:06:08,814 --> 00:06:10,856
Steel is next.

108
00:06:10,806 --> 00:06:13,631
About 70 percent of American steel is

109
00:06:13,631 --> 00:06:16,456
made by melting scrap in electric furnaces.

110
00:06:16,456 --> 00:06:18,867
But making new steel from iron ore

111
00:06:18,867 --> 00:06:20,933
relies on coke and natural gas.

112
00:06:21,452 --> 00:06:24,684
Use clean hydrogen to pull the oxygen out of the

113
00:06:24,684 --> 00:06:27,915
ore instead, and the Energy Department says the life-cycle emissions

114
00:06:27,915 --> 00:06:31,147
of new steel could drop by 40 to 70 percent.

115
00:06:31,618 --> 00:06:33,851
Then come the maybes.

116
00:06:33,801 --> 00:06:36,295
Ships could run on fuels made

117
00:06:36,295 --> 00:06:38,788
from hydrogen, like ammonia and methanol.

118
00:06:38,788 --> 00:06:42,195
By mid-2025, the IEA counted more than

119
00:06:42,195 --> 00:06:45,115
60 methanol-powered vessels on the water.

120
00:06:45,624 --> 00:06:48,342
Heavy trucks are another maybe.

121
00:06:48,292 --> 00:06:51,424
The Energy Department's strategy says fuel cells are

122
00:06:51,424 --> 00:06:54,556
particularly viable for trucks that need fill times

123
00:06:54,556 --> 00:06:57,297
like diesel, or ranges above 500 miles.

124
00:06:57,806 --> 00:07:01,233
And the grid could store hydrogen for days or even weeks,

125
00:07:01,233 --> 00:07:04,659
the EIA says, while the average U.S. grid battery in 2019

126
00:07:04,659 --> 00:07:07,463
held about an hour and a half of output.

127
00:07:07,413 --> 00:07:11,175
The country already stores hydrogen in three underground caverns, and

128
00:07:11,175 --> 00:07:14,938
in Utah, a project backed by a federal loan guarantee

129
00:07:14,938 --> 00:07:18,701
is designed to pair a 220-megawatt electrolyzer with salt caverns.

130
00:07:19,654 --> 00:07:22,436
Which brings us back to the billions.

131
00:07:22,386 --> 00:07:25,964
The 2021 infrastructure law gave the Energy

132
00:07:25,964 --> 00:07:29,031
Department 9.5 billion dollars for hydrogen.

133
00:07:29,031 --> 00:07:32,527
In 2023, the Energy Department selected seven regional

134
00:07:32,527 --> 00:07:36,023
hydrogen hubs, with 7 billion dollars in funding,

135
00:07:36,023 --> 00:07:39,082
to put producers and users close together.

136
00:07:39,588 --> 00:07:43,106
Its national strategy aims for 10 million metric tons

137
00:07:43,106 --> 00:07:46,623
of clean hydrogen a year by 2030, 20 million

138
00:07:46,623 --> 00:07:49,359
by 2040, and 50 million by 2050.

139
00:07:49,826 --> 00:07:52,506
But plans have met reality.

140
00:07:52,456 --> 00:07:56,402
In 2025, the IEA reported that planned projects in North America

141
00:07:56,402 --> 00:08:00,349
had shrunk, mostly in the U.S., amid policy uncertainty, and that

142
00:08:00,349 --> 00:08:03,578
the future of four of the hubs was unclear.

143
00:08:04,546 --> 00:08:06,371
Quick recap.

144
00:08:06,321 --> 00:08:09,510
Hydrogen is a carrier, not a source.

145
00:08:09,510 --> 00:08:13,113
America makes most of it from natural gas, and turning

146
00:08:13,113 --> 00:08:16,716
electricity into hydrogen and back loses most of the energy.

147
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:19,694
That's why it struggles in cars

148
00:08:19,694 --> 00:08:22,156
and homes, where electricity works directly.

149
00:08:22,106 --> 00:08:24,677
But in refineries, fertilizer and steel,

150
00:08:24,677 --> 00:08:27,247
the molecule itself is the point.

151
00:08:27,758 --> 00:08:32,198
Hydrogen isn't a fuel problem, it's a where-do-you-use-it problem.

152
00:08:32,148 --> 00:08:35,024
So where would you use it: trucks, ships,

153
00:08:35,024 --> 00:08:37,900
steel, or storing power for a windless week?

154
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:40,056
Tell me in the comments.

155
00:08:40,564 --> 00:08:43,549
If you like seeing how the hidden machinery of

156
00:08:43,549 --> 00:08:46,203
everyday life works, subscribe to The Hidden Grid.

157
00:08:46,153 --> 00:08:48,264
Every source behind the numbers in

158
00:08:48,264 --> 00:08:50,375
this video is in the description.
